---
title: Ploceidae
---
# Ploceidae

Ploceidae is the weaver family of Africa and southern Asia, about a hundred and
twenty species of small, thick-billed songbirds. The male plaits a roofed
chamber for the eggs out of strips torn from palm or reed leaves, twining and
knotting each strip against the last around the tip of a slender branch until
the whole structure is a fabric. The chamber is slung beneath the branch and
entered from underneath, so that it swings free in the wind with nothing
supporting it from below, and a snake working along the limb cannot reach it.
A female tugs at the plaiting to test it; a loose one is abandoned and the male
takes it apart. See [[village-weaver]].
